  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Using Block: Vorbereitung Wurzel 05</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Solve the equation \(z^4 = 1\). How many solutions are there? Add as many input fields as you need. Enter your solutions in cartesian form.</p>
<p>
[[repeat id="1"]]
\(z_{[[repeatindex/]]} = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]<br>
[[/repeat]]
[[repeatbutton title="Add another root" repeat_ids="1" save_state="state1" /]]
[[input:state1]] [[validation:state1]]
</p>
[[feedback:prt1]]]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Solution</strong>: The equation \(z^4 = 1\) has as many solutions as the degree of the polynomial, here \(4\).  So press the button three times, to get four input fields.</p>
<p>All the solutions have the same absolute value, and each one is the previous one rotated anticlockwise by \(\pi/2\).  Once we have found a first solution, we get all the others quickly.</p>
<p>The first solution is easy: of course \(1^4 = 1\), so \(z_1 = 1\).  Now we rotate, and write the results in cartesian form:</p>\[z_2 = z_1 \cdot e^{\text{i}\pi/2} = 1 \cdot \text{i} = \text{i},\]\[z_3 = z_2 \cdot e^{\text{i}\pi/2} = \text{i} \cdot \text{i} = -1,\]\[ z_4 = z_3 \cdot e^{\text{i}\pi/2} = -1 \cdot \text{i} = -\text{i}.\]
<p>The four solutions form a square.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0</penalty>
